3.2 Selection and preparation of the MexB protein structure 

The three-dimensional structure of the MexB multidrug efflux transporter from Pseudomonas aeruginosa was selected from the Protein Data Bank (PDB) using the structure 3W9I. The biological assembly containing the MexB homotrimer was used for subsequent structural analysis. The three protomers were designated according to their conformational states as Access (chain A), Binding (chain B), and Extrusion (chain C). 

The structure was inspected and processed to obtain suitable coordinate files for computational analysis. The three conformational states were retained separately to allow comparison of structural changes associated with the functional transport cycle (Ryosuke  et al.,2013). 

3.3 Conformational-state analysis of MexB 

To characterize the structural differences between the Access, Binding, and Extrusion states, the transmembrane domains (TMDs) of the three MexB protomers were used as structural alignment references. The TMD regions were selected because they provide a relatively conserved structural framework, allowing differences in the porter domains to be examined independently of overall protomer orientation (Helen et al.,2000) 

The Access, Binding, and Extrusion structures were aligned using their corresponding TMD regions. Root-mean-square deviation (RMSD) values were calculated to assess the structural similarity of the TMD regions following superposition. 

The relative movement of the porter domains was subsequently evaluated by measuring distances between selected PN1–PN2 and PC1–PC2 regions in each conformational state. This analysis was used to characterize the progressive opening and closing of the porter domain during the Access → Binding → Extrusion transition. 

3.4 Identification of functionally relevant residues 

Residues associated with the MexB proton-relay mechanism were examined to ensure that important functional regions of the transporter were retained correctly in the structural model. Particular attention was given to ASP407, ASP408, LYS939, and THR976, which are located within the proton-relay/transmembrane region (Satoshi et al., 2006). 

The analysis also examined the phenylalanine-rich regions of the porter domain because aromatic residues can contribute to ligand recognition and stabilization within the internal binding cavities. 

3.5 Identification of ligand-binding pockets using P2Rank 

Potential ligand-binding pockets in the MexB structure were identified using P2Rank 2.5.1, a machine-learning-based binding-site prediction method (Radoslav  et al., 2018). 

The aligned MexB conformational structures were submitted for pocket prediction, and the predicted pocket locations, scores, probabilities, and associated residues were examined. Based on the predicted pocket location and its position within the porter domain, the Deep Binding Pocket (DBP) in the Binding state was selected as the principal docking site for ligand screening. 

3.6 Definition of the Deep Binding Pocket 

The DBP was defined using the P2Rank-predicted pocket coordinates. A docking box was subsequently constructed around the predicted DBP to encompass the pocket and its surrounding ligand-accessible region (Jerome et al., 2021). 

