These residues were used as structural markers to determine whether docked ligands occupied the intended DBP region (Jerome et al., 2021). 

3.8 Ligand dataset preparation 

A ligand dataset consisting of candidate compounds associated with MexB-related screening was assembled. The ligands were prepared in PDBQT format for molecular docking. 

The ligand preparation workflow included obtaining the ligand structures, converting them into appropriate three-dimensional representations, assigning the required molecular properties for docking, and generating individual PDBQT files (Jerome et al., 2021). 

A total of 30 candidate ligands were prepared and subjected to docking against the defined MexB DBP. 

3.9 Molecular docking of candidate ligands 

Molecular docking was performed using AutoDock Vina v1.2.7. Each of the 30 candidate ligands was independently docked into the defined DBP of the MexB Binding-state structure. 

The same receptor, docking grid, exhaustiveness, number of output modes, energy range, and random seed were used for all compounds (Jerome et al., 2021). 

For each ligand, the docking program generated multiple possible binding poses. The pose with the most favorable predicted binding affinity (lowest/more negative Vina binding energy) was selected as the primary pose for subsequent analysis. 

